#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087578 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087851 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9d8d4d2-fef7-5ada-8691-3f504cb3cf97" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degrees of the Numerator and Denominator are Equal"}] :question/id #uuid "eb0b899e-af3a-587c-96ae-f1da9e0929dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-5847" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{4x^3-x}{3x^3+24}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^3.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}-x}{3{x}^{3}+24} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{24}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(4-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(3+\\frac{24}{{x}^{3}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ and $\\dfrac{24}{x^3}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(4-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(3+\\frac{24}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{4-0}{3+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y = \\dfrac{4}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087579 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087852 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f75252e-bf7d-59ed-bb16-6a2b65a97863" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Denominator is Greater"}] :question/id #uuid "0d53819b-ca24-549d-98e2-a9b78b49c080" :question/math-academy-id "e-5849" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{3x^2+ 2x}{6x^4-1}$." :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^4.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x}{6{x}^{4}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(6-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms  $\\dfrac{3}{x^2}, \\dfrac{2}{x^3},$ and $\\dfrac{1}{x^4}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(6-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{6-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y = 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087580 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "653503b2-4631-561e-af5e-847bcbdfe8e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Numerator is Greater"}] :question/id #uuid "ea2a97a7-fce8-5b4a-a269-57e0ae7984d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5851" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x) = \\dfrac{2x^3}{x^2-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^2.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the term $\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ becomes very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{2x}{1-0} \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y = f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "653503b2-4631-561e-af5e-847bcbdfe8e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Numerator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f3bea2c-306c-5e48-88e0-f498569d09ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378424} {:answer/id #uuid "10399da4-e690-5c4d-b2a4-9bff2c1171d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378425} {:answer/id #uuid "a1078051-d4e1-5037-957d-9cb4bee113ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378426} {:answer/id #uuid "0308c1f2-6766-5c2d-93c2-954e116c5e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378427} {:answer/id #uuid "c831d6cb-31f4-5404-8eed-6b17d2e24673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10399da4-e690-5c4d-b2a4-9bff2c1171d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378425} :answer-field/id #uuid "219e25d5-1a5d-54bd-84e6-7016995fdf31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4507ae6a-461d-5502-b3e2-7bf57bad8732" :question/math-academy-id "q-113180" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3x+2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x})}{(\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(3+\\frac{2}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the term $\\frac{2}{x}$ becomes very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(3+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434789 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087851 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9d8d4d2-fef7-5ada-8691-3f504cb3cf97" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degrees of the Numerator and Denominator are Equal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bea34e5-3538-5b53-9d1b-3da84e003295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bea34e5-3538-5b53-9d1b-3da84e003295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434776} :answer-field/id #uuid "645dd210-943e-5360-a7a2-cfed7ab075c6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eabf075e-fdbb-5a7f-b63d-7f2c28ee58cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-277948" :question/problem "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{7x-9{x}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}+8x-5}$ is $y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn this case, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{7x-9{x}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}+8x-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{8x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{x}-9)}{(3+\\frac{8}{x}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{7}{x},$ $\\frac{8}{x},$ and $\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{x}-9)}{(3+\\frac{8}{x}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0-9}{3+0-0} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087851 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9d8d4d2-fef7-5ada-8691-3f504cb3cf97" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degrees of the Numerator and Denominator are Equal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94c42b4e-7ee9-504e-8b91-1700f6daf898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94c42b4e-7ee9-504e-8b91-1700f6daf898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434779} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b5d6234-c7ef-58da-94fa-691c00244659" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "030d4922-51e9-524a-b5f1-bb14227873b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-277952" :question/problem "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x(4{x}^{2}+7)}{6{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}}$ is $y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The given function can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{12{x}^{3}+21x}{6{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{12{x}^{3}+21x}{6{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{12{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{21x}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(12+\\frac{21}{{x}^{2}})}{(6-\\frac{5}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{21}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{5}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(12+\\frac{21}{{x}^{2}})}{(6-\\frac{5}{x})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{12+0}{6-0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087852 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f75252e-bf7d-59ed-bb16-6a2b65a97863" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Denominator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ec2ab89-ebdf-50c8-bf29-dc40d7200760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1,\\,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423002} {:answer/id #uuid "bfdb7b5d-ee60-569b-b40a-75a261258200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423012} {:answer/id #uuid "c06ff70c-7a27-5b2e-868a-5637f1729306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423013} {:answer/id #uuid "cbb0d71f-1a47-548f-bfbd-33f93735b69d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423016} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b97868-31e1-5731-98b7-826c964228e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c06ff70c-7a27-5b2e-868a-5637f1729306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423013} :answer-field/id #uuid "12081ca9-74ed-57c2-90fb-17d28aa1c69d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a1258d96-ffcb-5d05-bab8-0035cb3a121b" :question/math-academy-id "q-4002" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+5}{{x}^{2}-x-2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+5}{{x}^{2}-x-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{1}{x},\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{1-0-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434788 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087852 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f75252e-bf7d-59ed-bb16-6a2b65a97863" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Denominator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14c1c570-e286-5d61-a7d1-a10aa9c6de44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434770} {:answer/id #uuid "23025a8b-6786-567b-bd9a-eb8e19beee8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434771} {:answer/id #uuid "2c78aa54-0ac9-53df-b432-2d5fc7834c15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434772} {:answer/id #uuid "934b0a62-a4f3-5bdb-bdb5-7947c45a87a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434778} {:answer/id #uuid "ae9e71d4-cc83-5871-a1ab-c17e54f55ec1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14c1c570-e286-5d61-a7d1-a10aa9c6de44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434770} :answer-field/id #uuid "40038cf6-6965-5518-9d52-0ed4bca4de62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ea2595d-82f6-56e5-b377-9d57e3c87c0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-6885" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{5{x}^{2}+2}{{x}^{4}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}+2}{{x}^{4}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{1-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "653503b2-4631-561e-af5e-847bcbdfe8e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Numerator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fea007a-0dd4-5f6c-9257-ed12e9d45202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\,\\frac{1}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434773} {:answer/id #uuid "3576ce64-1716-532c-9b43-4e79dd447ff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\,\\frac{7}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434774} {:answer/id #uuid "50d16fda-27d0-5acd-9321-077265d3dfcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434775} {:answer/id #uuid "81a8f7bc-4cfa-55bc-a03d-5635c11ef30b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434777} {:answer/id #uuid "bf44ca15-0e20-5367-bb70-8f8828b77a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{10}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50d16fda-27d0-5acd-9321-077265d3dfcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434775} :answer-field/id #uuid "01e0e053-f6d5-5da3-8737-b2a31b0bda6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "240f8824-faa1-5120-9751-29976f966dc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-6994" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{(2-x)(x+5)}{17x+7}.$" :question/worked-solution "The given function can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{10-3x-{x}^{2}}{17x+7}\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{10-3x-{x}^{2}}{17x+7} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{10}{x}-\\frac{3x}{x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x})}{(\\frac{17x}{x}+\\frac{7}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{10}{x}-3-x)}{(17+\\frac{7}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{10}{x}$ and $\\frac{7}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{10}{x}-3-x)}{(17+\\frac{7}{x})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0-3-x}{17+0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x+3}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."}]}
